  "names": {
    "order": "given family",
    "conventions": "Ovambo names, the most common in the cities, are usually an Oshiwambo name with a meaning (Ndapewa, I have been given; Tuhafeni, let us rejoice) carried with a baptismal name. Many of those baptismal names are Finnish, from the Lutheran missionaries in the north (Toivo, Aune, Lahja, Selma). Ovambo surnames often go back to a forebear's name (Shikongo, Nangolo, Iipumbu). Many Nama and Damara people carry Afrikaans or biblical first names; Damara and many Nama family names change with gender, ending in -b for a man and -s for a woman (Gariseb and Garises), so only the forms that do not change are listed here. Afrikaans names belong to Afrikaners, the coloured community and the Rehoboth Basters, and German names to the small German-speaking community. Herero, Kavango and Zambezi names are not yet listed. Older people are greeted as Tate (father) and Meme (mother) whatever their background.",
